<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">Yamashina Hongan-ji</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1483–1532</span></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Yamashina Hongan-ji was the head temple of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jodo-shinshu">True Pure Land school</a> of Japanese <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buddhism">Buddhism</a>, built by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rennyo">Rennyo</a> at Yamashina, east of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyoto">Kyoto</a>, and completed around 1483. A fortified temple town grew around it, from which the sect's leaders directed their followers, including the leagues ruling Kaga. It was burned in 1532 by rival warlords and Nichiren-sect townsmen, and the leadership moved to Ishiyama.</p></div>
